  "narrative": "All twelve new analyses are valid Welch comparisons, but none provides evidence of a threshold-level effect modification in the claimed direction. The interaction screen among feature_010, feature_002, and feature_037 yields raw modification magnitudes only about 0.02 to 0.08 composite dependency-score units, far below the 0.15 threshold. H59 (R53) and H60/H62 (R54/R56) are signed in the opposite direction and their upper bounds remain below +0.15, so I reject those claims and close them. The reciprocal feature_010-feature_002 interaction H75 (R57: +0.077; interval -0.042 to 0.196) and the feature_010-feature_037 interactions H61/H76 (R55/R58: +0.070; interval -0.053 to 0.192) have point estimates well below threshold; their upper bounds cross +0.15 only because the intervals are wide, not because the estimates support meaningful modification. I therefore leave these as unresolved but defer them rather than prioritize validation, because the observed modification is small and the reciprocal null evidence does not justify a stronger subgroup interpretation. The categorical screen is also largely negative. feature_046 level_002 versus level_001 (H67, R59: +0.083; upper +0.144) is statistically positive but excludes the 0.15 threshold, so I reject it as scientifically meaningful in this dataset. feature_046 level_003 versus level_001 (H68, R60: +0.127; interval -0.016 to +0.269) is the only candidate near threshold, but its lower bound is not positive and its point estimate is below threshold, so it remains unresolved and deferred rather than accepted or validated now. feature_059 and feature_054 level contrasts (H69, H77, H78; R61/R63/R64) are small or opposite to the claimed direction and upper bounds are well below +0.15, so they are rejected. H70 (R62) has only 25 observations in the exposed cell, a near-zero estimate, and a very wide interval, so I mark it unresolved but deferred. No validation is requested because no new discovery interval has a lower bound approaching the claimed-direction threshold, and strict independent intervals would be unlikely to convert these low-point estimates into decisive acceptances. The main interpretation remains that feature_010, feature_002, and feature_037 have large, separately validated associations with outcome_001 and appear approximately additive across one another within this dataset; the results do not imply causality, biological mechanism, or external generalizability, and joint adjustment for latent structure remains unassessed.",
  "research_notes": "Updated Phase 5 assessment: pairwise interaction screen across the three core binary predictors is negative overall. Point interaction estimates are small (about 0.02-0.08 composite dependency-score units) and none has a lower bound above +0.15. H59, H60, and H62 are closed rejects. Reciprocal or positive interaction intervals H61, H75, and H76 cross the +0.15 threshold only at their upper limits but point estimates are below threshold; they are deferred unresolved, not accepted. Core conclusions: feature_010=1 and feature_002=1 remain associated with higher outcome_001 (weaker dependency), and feature_037=1 with lower outcome_001 (stronger dependency), all validated, with conditional mean differences supporting pairwise persistence. Current categorical screen: feature_046 level_002 versus level_001 is below threshold and rejected; feature_046 level_003 versus level_001 is uncertain and deferred; feature_059 and feature_054 screened contrasts are rejected except sparse H70 deferred. No validation requested now because candidates have lower bounds far from the claimed-direction threshold and strict validation intervals are unlikely to be decisive; reserve validation for a future claim with a discovery estimate and lower bound much closer to +0.15. Next priorities: complete remaining registered categorical contrasts (H71-H74, H79-H80) if they are informative, and possibly revisit deferred sparse binary screen H38-H49 only after stronger categorical or interaction candidates are exhausted. All claims remain dataset-level associations without causal interpretation."
}

